insert into lessons (module_id, title, slug, content, order_index)
values ((select id from modules where slug = 'algebraic-techniques' and track_id = (select id from tracks where slug = 'aime')), 'Lifting the Exponent Lemma', 'lifting-the-exponent-lemma', '[{"type":"meta","author":"Divyanshu Upreti","frequency":"supplemental"},{"type":"section","title":"Introduction"},{"type":"text","content":"The **Lifting the Exponent** lemma (LTE) computes the exact power of a prime $p$ dividing expressions like $a^n - b^n$. Write $v_p(N)$ for the **$p$-adic valuation** of $N$ — the exponent of $p$ in the prime factorization of $N$. LTE turns a hard factoring question into a one-line formula."},{"type":"section","title":"Statement"},{"type":"text","content":"Let $p$ be an **odd** prime and let $a, b$ be integers not divisible by $p$, with $p \\mid (a - b)$. Then for every positive integer $n$,\n$$v_p\\!\\left(a^n - b^n\\right) = v_p(a - b) + v_p(n).$$\n\nThere is a companion for sums: if $p \\mid (a+b)$ and $n$ is **odd**, then\n$$v_p\\!\\left(a^n + b^n\\right) = v_p(a + b) + v_p(n).$$\n\nThe prime $p = 2$ needs a separate, slightly different statement."},{"type":"section","title":"Proof Sketch"},{"type":"text","content":"The base case is the factorization\n$$a^n - b^n = (a - b)\\left(a^{n-1} + a^{n-2}b + \\cdots + b^{n-1}\\right).$$\nWhen $p \\nmid n$, the second factor is $\\equiv n a^{n-1} \\not\\equiv 0 \\pmod p$ (using $a \\equiv b$), so it contributes no extra factors of $p$ and $v_p(a^n - b^n) = v_p(a-b)$.\n\nTo handle a factor of $p$ in $n$, one shows $v_p(a^p - b^p) = v_p(a - b) + 1$ by expanding $a = b + pk$ with the binomial theorem. Writing $n = p^t m$ with $p \\nmid m$ and iterating gives the $+\\,v_p(n)$ term."},{"type":"resource","source":"AoPS","stars":5,"title":"Lifting the Exponent Lemma","description":"Full statement (including the p = 2 case), a complete proof, and olympiad problems.","url":"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Lifting_the_Exponent"},{"type":"section","title":"Worked AIME-Style Examples"},{"type":"problem","title":"Power of 3 in a difference","source":"AIME (practice)","difficulty":"Medium","statement":"Find the largest power of $3$ dividing $10^{27} - 1$.","hint":"Apply LTE with $p = 3$, $a = 10$, $b = 1$. Check that $3 \\mid (a - b)$ first.","solution":"Here $a - b = 9$, so $v_3(a-b) = 2$, and $v_3(27) = 3$. By LTE,\n$$v_3\\!\\left(10^{27} - 1\\right) = v_3(9) + v_3(27) = 2 + 3 = 5.$$\nSo $3^5 = 243$ is the largest power of $3$ dividing the number."},{"type":"problem","title":"A divisibility count","source":"AIME (practice)","difficulty":"Hard","statement":"Let $N = 7^{2024} - 1$. Find $v_2(N) + v_3(N)$, the total number of factors of $2$ and $3$ in $N$.","hint":"For $p=3$ use standard LTE. For $p = 2$, use the even version: $v_2(a^n - b^n) = v_2(a-b) + v_2(a+b) + v_2(n) - 1$ when $n$ is even.","solution":"**Prime $3$:** with $a = 7,\\ b = 1$, we have $v_3(a-b) = v_3(6) = 1$ and $v_3(2024) = 0$, so $v_3(N) = 1$.\n\n**Prime $2$:** since $2024$ is even, the $p=2$ formula gives\n$$v_2(N) = v_2(7-1) + v_2(7+1) + v_2(2024) - 1 = 1 + 3 + 3 - 1 = 6,$$\nbecause $2024 = 2^3 \\cdot 11 \\cdot 23$. Hence $v_2(N) + v_3(N) = 6 + 1 = 7.$"}]'::jsonb, 0)
on conflict (module_id, slug) do update set
